      <p class="lede">
        A trim panel off by half a millimeter can stop an assembly line — tracking down exactly why is the part of engineering I enjoy most. I work at the intersection of <strong>mechanical design</strong>, <strong>manufacturing quality</strong>, and <strong>sustainable engineering</strong>: three disciplines that all come down to the same question — how do you close the gap between what a drawing specifies and what a factory produces, reliably, at scale?
        <br><br>
        Six months inside Tata Motors' Trim Chassis Final shop gave me hands-on experience in <strong>Fit-to-Nominal (FTN) analysis</strong>, <strong>CMM metrology</strong>, <strong>Poka-Yoke error-proofing</strong>, <strong>NVH and EBHS testing</strong>, and <strong>PLM-linked quality documentation</strong> across a live new-vehicle programme. A year of B.Tech research built on top of that: Grey-Taguchi design of experiments on a fully instrumented engine test bench, an ANN surrogate model with 90%+ prediction accuracy, and a peer-reviewed publication on sustainable alternative fuels.
        <br><br>
        I'm now finishing an MSc specialising in Smart &amp; Sustainable Industry at Politecnico di Milano — combining <strong>sustainable manufacturing processes</strong>, <strong>materials and damage analysis</strong>, and <strong>machine vision &amp; advanced measurements</strong> with the practical foundation I built on the shop floor. I'm looking for a product development or manufacturing engineering team in Lombardy and northern Italy where precision, continuous improvement, and engineering rigour actually matter.
